{"timestamp":"2026-08-06T11:12:29.229Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_019fd6c6-522d-76d0-99d4-ddfb469de25a","author":"/root/complete_manifest","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/complete_manifest\nPayload:\nThe 99 committed layers are submission-ready structurally: all are nonempty, single-parent, under 100 files, and focused. No merge commits or mixed-concern layers were found.\n\nThe pending timeout-wrapper branch is the only caveat: `codex/timeout-wrapper-cleanup-reporting` still points to position 99, so its committed branch diff is currently empty. Its uncommitted two-file diff is focused at +129/-5 and is represented below as the proposed position 100.\n\n## Positions 1–25\n\n1. `codex/remove-prior-compliance-artifacts`  \n   Title: `chore: remove prior compliance artifacts`  \n   Removes the superseded compliance report and one-off audit scripts. Updates active quality documentation so it no longer references those artifacts.\n\n2. `codex/remove-dead-runtime-dependencies`  \n   Title: `refactor: remove dead runtime dependencies`  \n   Removes unused browser-auth, tax-rate logging, and icon-experiment helpers. Aligns runtime configuration, dependencies, and documentation with the supported surface.\n\n3. `codex/remove-legacy-migration-tools`  \n   Title: `refactor: remove legacy migration runner`  \n   Deletes the legacy migration entrypoint, preflight, cutover guard, and tests. Removes its package wiring and obsolete repository guidance.\n\n4. `codex/remove-tenant-migration-steps`  \n   Title: `refactor: remove tenant migration steps`  \n   Deletes obsolete organization, tax-rate, and permission migration steps. Removes the tests dedicated to those retired operations.\n\n5. `codex/remove-identity-migration-steps`  \n   Title: `refactor: remove identity migration steps`  \n   Deletes legacy user, role, assignment, and application-manager migration code. Removes the matching migration tests.\n\n6. `codex/remove-event-migration-steps`  \n   Title: `refactor: remove event migration steps`  \n   Deletes retired event, template, and category import steps. The layer remains limited to the old event migration surface.\n\n7. `codex/remove-legacy-event-mapping-rules`  \n   Title: `refactor: remove legacy event mapping rules`  \n   Removes legacy mapping helpers for event location, pricing, publication, registration, roles, and timestamps. Deletes their migration-focused tests.\n\n8. `codex/remove-migration-support-utilities`  \n   Title: `refactor: remove migration support utilities`  \n   Deletes retired migration configuration, database adapters, and icon tooling. Adjusts the remaining authority-source checks accordingly.\n\n9. `codex/remove-migration-repository-wiring`  \n   Title: `refactor: remove migration repository wiring`  \n   Removes migration source-root and dependency wiring from application tooling. Updates guidance and quality checks so they no longer expect the deleted tree.\n\n10. `codex/remove-legacy-schema-module`  \n    Title: `refactor: remove legacy schema module`  \n    Deletes the obsolete Drizzle configuration and legacy schema modules. Leaves the current data model as the only active schema surface.\n\n11. `codex/remove-legacy-schema-history`  \n    Title: `refactor: remove legacy schema history`  \n    Deletes the retired initial SQL migration and journal metadata. No active schema implementation is changed.\n\n12. `codex/remove-generated-legacy-snapshot`  \n    Title: `refactor: remove generated legacy snapshot`  \n    Deletes the generated snapshot belonging to the retired schema history. The large deletion is one generated file, not mixed implementation work.\n\n13. `codex/remove-invalid-types-alias`  \n    Title: `refactor: remove invalid types alias`  \n    Removes the unsupported `@types/*` path alias and adopts direct imports. Documents the convention and adjusts affected checks.\n\n14. `codex/guard-local-drizzle-targets`  \n    Title: `fix: keep local database commands on one target`  \n    Adds a fail-closed preflight for local database operations. Routes Drizzle and PostgreSQL integration processes through the same validated target.\n\n15. `codex/require-explicit-seed-context`  \n    Title: `fix: require explicit atomic seed context`  \n    Requires seed helpers to receive coherent organization, event, account, and transaction context. Updates fixtures to pass that context explicitly.\n\n16. `codex/fail-closed-local-seeding`  \n    Title: `fix: fail closed local database reset`  \n    Stops reset and staging initialization when required seed information is absent or inconsistent. Consolidates supported PostgreSQL setup behavior.\n\n17. `codex/reuse-primary-provider-credentials`  \n    Title: `test: reuse primary provider credentials`  \n    Adds a helper for reusing approved location and membership-card test values. Missing values now produce an explicit failure instead of replacement configuration.\n\n18. `codex/harden-brand-asset-storage`  \n    Title: `fix: harden tenant brand asset storage`  \n    Validates branding uploads by organization, intended use, declared format, and actual content. Keeps storage failures visible and verifies saved assets can be read back.\n\n19. `codex/use-production-shaped-tenant-fixtures`  \n    Title: `test: use production-shaped tenant fixtures`  \n    Introduces a shared organization fixture with production-shaped identifiers and domain relationships. Uses it for database and parallel-test setup.\n\n20. `codex/fail-closed-local-runtime-state`  \n    Title: `test: make local runtime state fail closed`  \n    Validates generated runtime, authentication, cookie, and session-secret state. Tightens helper preflights and removes blanket HTTPS-error suppression.\n\n21. `codex/harden-container-runtime-image`  \n    Title: `build: harden container runtime image`  \n    Moves production execution to a pinned non-root distroless image with a direct Bun entrypoint. Restricts local bindings and strengthens image-content checks.\n\n22. `codex/enforce-registration-graph-integrity`  \n    Title: `fix: enforce registration graph integrity`  \n    Adds bounds for quantities, questions, and checkout lines across event and template graphs. Enforces ownership and protects questions with saved answers.\n\n23. `codex/settle-registration-addons-in-one-checkout`  \n    Title: `feat: settle registration add-ons in one checkout`  \n    Combines registration and selected add-ons into one bounded checkout with persisted prices. Aligns completion, expiry, refund, and fulfillment so retries reuse the reservation.\n\n24. `codex/define-registration-transfer-claim-rules`  \n    Title: `feat: define registration transfer claim rules`  \n    Defines eligibility, cancellation, pricing, state, and claim-code rules for private transfers. Adds shared policy helpers for answers, ownership history, and payment-backed claims.\n\n25. `codex/harden-transfer-refund-recovery`  \n    Title: `fix: harden transfer refund recovery`  \n    Locks current eligibility during transfer finalization and queues a full refund when a competing change wins. Aligns refund and registration handling with recoverable outcomes.\n\n## Positions 26–50\n\n26. `codex/guard-transfer-acquisition-data`  \n    Title: `fix: guard transfer acquisition data`  \n    Bounds transferred places, add-on quantities, and checkout lines. Persists complete ownership information for transferred answers.\n\n27. `codex/remove-direct-registration-reassignment`  \n    Title: `refactor: remove direct registration reassignment`  \n    Removes the old direct member-to-member handover contract. Leaves private offer-and-claim transfers as the supported member flow.\n\n28. `codex/use-private-registration-transfer-claims`  \n    Title: `feat: use private transfer claim codes`  \n    Replaces the earlier credential mechanism with private claim codes. Normalizes deadlines and pricing failures while preserving one active offer.\n\n29. `codex/add-private-transfer-claim-interface`  \n    Title: `feat: expose private registration transfer claims`  \n    Adds member-facing code entry and claim-page behavior. Extends the typed contract with bounded codes, URLs, and explicit claim outcomes.\n\n30. `codex/attach-organization-payment-account`  \n    Title: `feat: attach first organization payment account`  \n    Adds an operations service for attaching an organization’s first ready payment account. Blocks conflicting setup when configuration or unfinished payments already exist.\n\n31. `codex/prove-payment-account-attachment-serialization`  \n    Title: `test: verify payment account attachment serialization`  \n    Adds PostgreSQL coverage for concurrent account-attachment attempts. Registers the focused specification in the integration inventory.\n\n32. `codex/add-private-payment-account-setup-route`  \n    Title: `feat: expose private payment account setup`  \n    Adds an internal worker-only route for payment-account setup. Reuses the private-trigger boundary and stays unavailable from unintended runtime roles.\n\n33. `codex/refresh-default-application-theme`  \n    Title: `feat: refresh default application theme`  \n    Makes the indigo, slate, orange, and warm-neutral palette the default. Retains earlier colors as the explicit classic theme.\n\n34. `codex/report-client-failures-safely`  \n    Title: `fix: report client failures safely`  \n    Centralizes useful client-facing failure messages while bounding browser diagnostics. Applies the boundary across global handling, calls, forms, and representative features.\n\n35. `codex/simplify-shared-form-controls`  \n    Title: `refactor: simplify shared form controls`  \n    Simplifies shared role, location, icon, and rich-text controls. Consolidates labels and removes obsolete control dependencies.\n\n36. `codex/clarify-application-access-boundaries`  \n    Title: `fix: clarify application access boundaries`  \n    Sends authenticated but unauthorized navigation to an explanatory no-access page. Aligns guards, internal routes, navigation, and legal-page access.\n\n37. `codex/serialize-and-bound-ticket-check-in`  \n    Title: `fix: serialize and bound ticket check-in`  \n    Serializes concurrent check-in changes and defines explicit availability windows. Carries those outcomes through contracts and administrator scanning.\n\n38. `codex/make-scanner-recovery-explicit`  \n    Title: `fix: make scanner recovery explicit`  \n    Checks scanner access before starting the camera and presents task-specific recovery actions. Removes the incomplete recent-ticket support list.\n\n39. `codex/use-plain-scanner-and-addon-language`  \n    Title: `refactor: use plain scanner and add-on language`  \n    Replaces internal terms with clear ticket, sign-up, place, item, and refund language. Adds task-specific recovery messages across scanning and fulfillment.\n\n40. `codex/harden-ticket-qr-responses`  \n    Title: `fix: harden ticket QR responses`  \n    Keeps QR responses private and distinguishes sign-in, missing-ticket, and organizer-help outcomes. Limits the change to the QR handler and its response coverage.\n\n41. `codex/validate-tenant-website-addresses`  \n    Title: `fix: validate tenant website addresses`  \n    Validates organization website addresses before deriving hosted origins. Aligns administrator forms, unknown-organization responses, and generated guidance.\n\n42. `codex/explain-blocked-tenant-integrity-changes`  \n    Title: `fix: explain blocked tenant integrity changes`  \n    Replaces internal errors with actionable explanations for blocked currency or public-address changes. Covers missing organizations, concurrent changes, and dependent records.\n\n43. `codex/normalize-tenant-role-authority`  \n    Title: `refactor: normalize tenant role authority`  \n    Centralizes role validation, permission partitioning, ordering, and duplicate-name handling. Rejects platform-only permissions at organization role-write boundaries.\n\n44. `codex/harden-auth-identity-boundaries`  \n    Title: `fix: harden auth identity boundaries`  \n    Requires complete sign-in configuration, trusted addresses, stable identity details, and isolated cookies. Preserves visible failures instead of accepting fallback authority state.\n\n45. `codex/refresh-onboarding-identity-state`  \n    Title: `fix: refresh onboarding identity state`  \n    Requires a stable signed-in identity for onboarding status and requirements. Refreshes navigation after completion so profile access uses current session state.\n\n46. `codex/prepare-isolated-hosting-state-roots`  \n    Title: `infra: prepare isolated Scaleway state roots`  \n    Introduces separate bootstrap, staging, and production Terraform roots. Moves shared setup into bootstrap and extends verification for the new layout.\n\n47. `codex/cut-over-hosting-environment-state`  \n    Title: `infra: cut over Scaleway environment state`  \n    Moves staging and production workflows onto isolated state roots. Removes the former combined root and updates resources and hosting checks.\n\n48. `codex/fail-closed-release-gates`  \n    Title: `ci: make Scaleway release gates fail closed`  \n    Stops releases when required safety evidence is absent, stale, or unsuccessful. Prevents failed test artifacts from entering release packages.\n\n49. `codex/make-hosting-deployments-forward-only`  \n    Title: `fix: make Scaleway deployments forward-only`  \n    Removes automatic rollback behavior and requires a corrected forward release. Aligns deployment workflows, private operations, and operator guidance.\n\n50. `codex/align-local-secrets-with-hardened-runtime`  \n    Title: `fix: align local secrets with hardened runtime`  \n    Replaces the undersized local session secret with one meeting the runtime minimum. Reads the webhook signing secret through Bun for compatibility with the hardened image.\n\n## Positions 51–75\n\n51. `codex/require-explicit-organization-configuration`  \n    Title: `refactor: require explicit tenant configuration`  \n    Stops receipt review when required organization configuration is absent rather than choosing a fallback. Aligns shared configuration, request context, and finance validation around that rule.\n\n52. `codex/expose-focused-organization-settings-mutations`  \n    Title: `refactor: expose focused tenant settings mutations`  \n    Adds narrowly scoped mutations for independent organization settings concerns. Keeps validation and server handling local to each settings boundary.\n\n53. `codex/project-client-safe-organization-configuration`  \n    Title: `refactor: project client-safe tenant configuration`  \n    Defines an explicit client-safe configuration projection. Keeps server-only configuration outside the client contract.\n\n54. `codex/add-organization-registration-settings`  \n    Title: `feat: add organization and registration settings`  \n    Adds independently saved organization-detail and registration settings pages. Includes focused form models, identity handling, and component coverage.\n\n55. `codex/add-appearance-payment-and-legal-settings`  \n    Title: `feat: add appearance payment and legal settings`  \n    Adds focused appearance, payment, and legal settings pages. Keeps each page’s permissions, readiness state, and form behavior within its concern.\n\n56. `codex/route-focused-organization-settings-pages`  \n    Title: `refactor: route focused tenant settings pages`  \n    Routes the focused settings pages and removes the former general-settings monolith. Rewires configuration consumers to the new boundaries.\n\n57. `codex/prove-organization-configuration-boundaries`  \n    Title: `test: prove tenant configuration boundaries`  \n    Adds coverage for client configuration loading and focused settings behavior. Documents independent saves, dirty-state protection, and upload completion expectations.\n\n58. `codex/harden-role-and-member-administration`  \n    Title: `refactor: harden role and member administration`  \n    Adds consistent validation and visible failures across role and member administration. Aligns organization and platform interfaces with their typed server contracts.\n\n59. `codex/remove-obsolete-user-attributes`  \n    Title: `refactor: remove obsolete user attributes`  \n    Removes unused organizer-status account state from persistence and request context. Leaves organizer access derived from current confirmed registrations.\n\n60. `codex/validate-profile-identity-fields`  \n    Title: `fix: validate profile identity fields`  \n    Validates contact email, payout email, and international bank-account values. Applies the same rules during onboarding, account creation, profile editing, and server writes.\n\n61. `codex/recheck-registration-eligibility-on-writes`  \n    Title: `fix: recheck registration eligibility at every write`  \n    Rechecks current eligibility immediately before registration, approval, payment retry, and waitlist writes. Keeps waitlist places outside the active-registration limit until converted.\n\n62. `codex/refund-paid-signups-after-access-changes`  \n    Title: `fix: refund paid signups when access changes`  \n    Cancels and refunds paid sign-ups that lose access before payment completion. Records and communicates the resulting outcome explicitly.\n\n63. `codex/use-complete-template-authoring`  \n    Title: `refactor: use complete template authoring`  \n    Routes template creation and editing through the complete graph-based authoring flow. Removes active reliance on the older separate editor.\n\n64. `codex/remove-obsolete-simple-template-coverage`  \n    Title: `test: remove obsolete simple template coverage`  \n    Deletes the retired simple-template service specification. The layer contains only obsolete coverage removal.\n\n65. `codex/remove-obsolete-template-api`  \n    Title: `refactor: remove obsolete template api`  \n    Deletes the retired simple-template service and contract operations. Removes remaining server and source-check references to that interface.\n\n66. `codex/delete-retired-template-form-helpers`  \n    Title: `refactor: delete retired template form helpers`  \n    Deletes form schemas and utilities used only by the old template editor. Removes their dedicated tests and source guard.\n\n67. `codex/harden-receipt-evidence-storage`  \n    Title: `fix: harden receipt evidence storage`  \n    Keeps receipt-storage outages visible and validates stored evidence. Defers preview-link preparation until an approval is opened and safely handles orphan cleanup.\n\n68. `codex/validate-finance-receipt-contracts`  \n    Title: `fix: validate finance receipt contracts`  \n    Rejects impossible dates, unsupported amounts, oversized batches, and malformed payout details. Applies bounded value types at shared and server contract boundaries.\n\n69. `codex/enforce-finance-receipt-mutations`  \n    Title: `fix: enforce finance receipt mutations`  \n    Applies receipt validation during review and reimbursement mutations. Keeps failures actionable and uses the organization’s current website address in review messages.\n\n70. `codex/simplify-receipt-reimbursements`  \n    Title: `refactor: simplify receipt reimbursements`  \n    Finalizes uploads once, cleans abandoned work safely, and retains masked payout evidence. Shares the confirmation flow between organization and platform finance operations.\n\n71. `codex/improve-receipt-submission-finance-views`  \n    Title: `feat: improve receipt submission and finance views`  \n    Improves receipt submission, approval, refund, and transaction interfaces. Reuses clear receipt fields and keeps updating lists stable.\n\n72. `codex/make-email-delivery-single-dispatch`  \n    Title: `fix: make email delivery single dispatch`  \n    Dispatches each queued email once and leaves uncertain delivery outcomes visible. Removes automatic ambiguous retries that could duplicate messages.\n\n73. `codex/simplify-email-outbox-records-overview`  \n    Title: `refactor: simplify email outbox records and overview`  \n    Simplifies outbox persistence, leasing, contracts, and the platform overview. Prioritizes older incidents while retaining each organization’s reply-to address.\n\n74. `codex/focus-profile-account-overview`  \n    Title: `refactor: focus profile account overview`  \n    Reduces the profile overview to account-related information. Removes embedded receipt presentation now owned by a dedicated page.\n\n75. `codex/separate-profile-discounts`  \n    Title: `refactor: separate profile discounts`  \n    Moves discount and membership-card behavior into its own profile concern. Keeps provider-specific mapping and presentation outside the account overview.\n\n## Positions 76–99\n\n76. `codex/route-focused-profile-pages`  \n    Title: `refactor: route focused profile pages`  \n    Adds a profile shell with separate account, event, discount, and receipt routes. Defines responsive navigation and explicit focus behavior between sections.\n\n77. `codex/define-event-discovery-rules`  \n    Title: `docs: define event discovery rules`  \n    Records the product and architecture rules for ordinary event discovery. Separates sign-up-derived discovery from role-targeted announcements.\n\n78. `codex/make-templates-structure-only`  \n    Title: `refactor: make templates structure only`  \n    Removes discovery settings from reusable event templates. Leaves each published event’s current sign-up choices to determine ordinary discovery.\n\n79. `codex/derive-event-discovery-from-sign-up-options`  \n    Title: `refactor: derive event discovery from sign-up options`  \n    Derives event visibility from the choices a person can currently use. Removes obsolete generic listing state while preserving explicit direct-link outcomes.\n\n80. `codex/target-information-only-events-by-role`  \n    Title: `feat: target information-only events by role`  \n    Lets organizers select which roles can discover an information-only event. An empty selection remains link-only and neither assigns roles nor sends messages.\n\n81. `codex/harden-platform-administration-contracts`  \n    Title: `fix: harden platform administration contracts`  \n    Bounds platform audit results and event-question data. Rejects incomplete or oversized administration data visibly.\n\n82. `codex/prove-organization-administration-and-member-access`  \n    Title: `test: prove organization administration and member access`  \n    Adds journey coverage for settings, roles, member assignment, permissions, and platform operations. Keeps scenarios and permission matrices scoped to administration access.\n\n83. `codex/explain-organization-and-platform-administration`  \n    Title: `docs: explain organization and platform administration`  \n    Reworks executable guides for settings, email outbox, maps, and platform operations. Keeps the documentation aligned with the focused administration flows.\n\n84. `codex/prove-profile-discounts-and-onboarding-journeys`  \n    Title: `test: prove profile, discounts, and onboarding journeys`  \n    Adds journey coverage for account creation, profile editing, events, receipts, and discounts. Keeps shared helpers focused on profile-facing behavior.\n\n85. `codex/prove-event-discovery-approval-and-templates`  \n    Title: `test: prove event discovery, approval, and templates`  \n    Adds coverage for discovery eligibility, approvals, organizer sign-up, categories, and template authoring. Consolidates the associated journey helpers around those event lifecycle boundaries.\n\n86. `codex/explain-event-discovery-and-publishing`  \n    Title: `docs: explain event discovery and publishing`  \n    Rewrites executable guides for announcements, discovery, approval, and event management. Reflects the separation between ordinary events and role-targeted information.\n\n87. `codex/prove-registration-and-ticket-lifecycle`  \n    Title: `test: prove registration and ticket lifecycle`  \n    Covers free and paid registration, add-ons, cancellation, transfer, and negative states. Updates shared scenario helpers to reflect the current ticket lifecycle.\n\n88. `codex/prove-scanning-and-fulfillment-journeys`  \n    Title: `test: prove scanning and fulfillment journeys`  \n    Covers check-in and add-on fulfillment through the scanner. Updates executable scanning guides around the same bounded workflows.\n\n89. `codex/prove-finance-receipts-and-webhook-recovery`  \n    Title: `test: prove finance, receipts, and webhook recovery`  \n    Covers finance permissions, receipt submission and reimbursement, tax rates, and payment-event recovery. Updates the corresponding executable finance guides.\n\n90. `codex/publish-plain-language-generated-guides`  \n    Title: `test: publish plain-language generated guides`  \n    Tightens generated-guide language and publication behavior. Aligns reporting, attachments, screenshots, and account-creation documentation with the publication contract.\n\n91. `codex/guard-generated-guide-sources`  \n    Title: `test: guard generated guide sources`  \n    Adds source-level guards for generated documentation and onboarding journeys. Prevents retired wording and unsupported source patterns from returning.\n\n92. `codex/use-plain-application-language`  \n    Title: `refactor: use plain application language`  \n    Replaces implementation terminology with task-oriented wording across the application. The wide 83-file diff is a single coherent language pass, not mixed functionality.\n\n93. `codex/make-acceptance-failures-explicit`  \n    Title: `test: make acceptance failures explicit`  \n    Makes skipped, incomplete, and recovery behavior explicit in acceptance tooling. Aligns quality-source checks, test inventory, and load-recovery scenarios.\n\n94. `codex/bound-local-docker-lifecycle`  \n    Title: `fix: bound local Docker lifecycle commands`  \n    Adds one bounded, fail-visible path for local Docker lifecycle operations. Stops cleanup after the first failure and reports project state without retrying or rebuilding implicitly.\n\n95. `codex/full-application-simplification-review` — preserve PR [#123](https://github.com/evorto-app/app/pull/123)  \n    Title: `docs: record application review checkpoint`  \n    Adds the durable application review queue after the 94 lower implementation and documentation layers. Records findings, accepted complexity, settled decisions, and outstanding proof without changing runtime behavior.\n\n96. `codex/trace-staging-request-latency`  \n    Title: `perf: trace staging request latency`  \n    Adds bounded timing context across sign-in, request resolution, rendering, and event loading. Keeps diagnostic context constrained so personal values are not captured.\n\n97. `codex/add-bounded-staging-latency-probe`  \n    Title: `feat: add bounded staging latency probe`  \n    Adds a bounded request-measurement script and its focused source coverage. Preserves partial evidence across transport failures and reports final collection status explicitly.\n\n98. `codex/automate-staging-latency-evidence`  \n    Title: `ci: automate staging latency evidence`  \n    Adds scheduled and deployment-linked collection of staging latency evidence. Keeps report-only measurement separate from authoritative deployment readiness.\n\n99. `codex/staging-latency-runbooks` — preserve PR [#124](https://github.com/evorto-app/app/pull/124)  \n    Title: `docs: explain staging latency response`  \n    Adds the response guide, monitoring documentation, and quality guidance for latency evidence. Connects those runbooks to the lower tracing, probe, and workflow layers.\n\n## Proposed pending position 100\n\n100. `codex/timeout-wrapper-cleanup-reporting` — pending, not yet a committed stack layer  \n     Title: `fix: preserve timeout wrapper signal outcomes`  \n     Treats a permission-denied post-exit process-group probe as an explicit inability to verify cleanup while preserving SIGINT, SIGTERM, and timeout outcomes. Scope is limited to the wall-clock timeout helper and deterministic regression coverage; actual signal-delivery permission failures and unexpected errors remain loud.\n\nFlag: the branch ref currently equals position 99, so its committed own diff is empty and it is not yet part of `gh stack`. The working diff is focused at two files, +129/-5.\n\n## Proposed updated PR bodies\n\nPR #123 — `codex/full-application-simplification-review`:\n\n```markdown\nThis documentation-only position-95 layer adds `APPLICATION_REVIEW_QUEUE.md` as the durable checkpoint for the 94 lower application, infrastructure, and acceptance slices. It separates resolved findings and accepted complexity from outstanding environment-dependent proof without changing runtime behavior. The four staging-latency layers remain above this checkpoint in the stack.\n```\n\nPR #124 — `codex/staging-latency-runbooks`:\n\n```markdown\nThis position-99 layer closes the four-part staging-latency slice by documenting how to collect, interpret, and respond to bounded request evidence. It connects the response guide, quality guidance, and Scaleway runbooks to the lower tracing, probe, and workflow layers while keeping evidence collection report-only rather than making it a release gate. This PR remains the top committed layer until the focused timeout-wrapper follow-up is committed and stacked.\n```\n\nNo tests were run and no checkout, edit, commit, push, ref change, remote update, or PR mutation was performed.\n\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fd2d8-aa27-7b33-9da1-34e7e79b653d"}}}
