{"timestamp":"2026-08-06T12:21:29.601Z","type":"compacted","payload":{"first_window_id":"019f9f44-d3cb-70b3-a93f-404d8932db3c","message":"","previous_window_id":"019fd6bc-de2c-7b20-86bd-9f217f8d6554","replacement_history":[{"content":[{"text":"/goal Get the latest main and then run a FULL review of the application.\nYour job is to prepare a review queue in a persisted file and then work through the entire application. You have to look for any kind of bug, inconsistency, missing implementation and security issue.\nBut importantly, you are to identify any overly complicated implementation, list it in the persisted file and make a suggestion for simplification.\nWe want to stay safe but need to be pragmatic here, too much complexity will hurt the development in the future. What also MUST NOT appear is overly ambitious fallbacks, it is more important to surface defects than try to circumvent any kind of failure. Also we do not want any backwards compatibility code, this project will be deployed entirely fresh once it it done.\nMake sure to improve on this, we need to end up with a solid, stupid, simple and straightforward project that can be maintained by a single developer and does not hide defects.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019f9f44-fa7a-7c91-8314-273200a9f498","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f9f44-d543-74e3-a680-1ff7fc7838a1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Please remove all files left from previous scans and compliance checks\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019f9f47-9c78-7d21-a23e-4275fa88fcff","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"71c81a73-aba6-4e12-80f1-0aad457c4b4f"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"We can do the scan first, cancel the old ones\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019f9f48-2611-7602-b053-bc70f23c5165","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"71c81a73-aba6-4e12-80f1-0aad457c4b4f"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Please cancel the current scan, i will run it separately, I want you to focus on the manual review\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019f9f48-6074-7712-bffe-d5378e86a478","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"71c81a73-aba6-4e12-80f1-0aad457c4b4f"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Continue\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019f9fc0-1707-7481-8b59-0bee5bc1d9cd","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"480e92cc-1842-47c1-89df-3b706b0f9621"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Prepare an interactive overview of the findings and recommendations that makes it easy to understand our current situation\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019f9fe7-cef1-7ac2-8fa5-7be8221040b0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f9fe7-cea7-77b2-adfb-c2526934c613"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Please open a pr for this\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa242-355a-7560-8354-3ccf8a21583e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa242-1e2f-77f3-9b8a-7a6278dbba6a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Please check if we can update bun, they improved some of the angular support recently\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa269-c737-70f2-b49f-4920e09e0017","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa242-1e2f-77f3-9b8a-7a6278dbba6a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Please link the relevant bun commits\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa273-2d3f-7433-bf00-fd906ea51c99","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa242-1e2f-77f3-9b8a-7a6278dbba6a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Also send them to me here\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa274-9e78-7323-8c77-e8165553ea50","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa242-1e2f-77f3-9b8a-7a6278dbba6a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Please add a new theme (also make it the new default)\nThe previous two themes should be kept\nThis is the desired config\n\n  --primary-color=\"#4956C8\" \\\n  --secondary-color=\"#62677A\" \\\n  --tertiary-color=\"#FFB26B\" \\\n  --neutral-color=\"#F7F4ED\" \\\n  --neutral-variant-color=\"#72778A\" \\\n  --error-color=\"#B3261E\" \\\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa289-945a-7aa0-80a3-2d0816c7481a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa242-1e2f-77f3-9b8a-7a6278dbba6a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"What work and decisions remain now?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa34a-03c1-7fb1-8534-d5214f2f2eb5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa349-f207-73f3-8497-653bab78a465"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003ccodex_delegation\u003e\n  \u003csource_thread_id\u003e019fa330-fb1e-7ea1-b0e5-482856b99bf3\u003c/source_thread_id\u003e\n  \u003cinput\u003ePlease update the existing APPLICATION_REVIEW_QUEUE.md in your worktree to record the following decisions from the user, without implementing application code: (1) waitlist entries do not consume tenant active-registration limit; check only on conversion into a real registration. (2) One private offer-and-claim transfer flow; free/questionless claims complete immediately, payment-involving transfers remain asynchronous through Stripe; future cleanup should remove obsolete direct-reassignment promise/endpoint. (3) Template listing audience is already settled: default discovery copied to new events, templates have no visibility; no decision remains. (4) Optionless announcements get explicit selected tenant roles for normal discovery; no roles means link-only; it does not grant access/send notifications. (5) General settings become independently saved focused sections/pages inside familiar two-column settings layout; provider/payment its own restricted page; exact names later. (6) Profile retains small shared shell but substantial areas get pages; redesign sparse overview into meaningful landing page. (7) New registration plus add-ons is one Stripe checkout session; transfers same review/checkout pattern but fixed bundle with recipient eligibility/questions/current discounts rechecked; do not build generic backend checkout framework just to unify distinct post-payment recovery rules. Use apply_patch, inspect the diff, and send a concise report to this coordinator when finished, blocked, or needing a decision; say whether work is continuing.\u003c/input\u003e\n\u003c/codex_delegation\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa355-5f21-7d73-ac41-a72b0557ae35","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa355-5363-76f2-b702-51cc6f7d6b79"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Can you implement the new decisions and then tell me what still needs my review?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa359-cd34-7b03-b54a-52c614a531ac","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa359-c120-7171-8d51-6fb44bae2fe2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"THere was some interference with docker and the tests, double check your setup and try again if something failed\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa3b4-8d32-7f33-86e1-096e77dd71a0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa359-c120-7171-8d51-6fb44bae2fe2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Also check up on the pr\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa408-cc56-7870-9c57-562f956faa1c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa408-c0e3-7741-b52d-66c4e756e051"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Please continue\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa422-619e-7b60-88f3-aa73262a7145","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa422-5063-7d01-8ee3-49440fc14bad"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"::codex-realtime-inline{}\n\nUpdate `PRODUCT.md` and `APPLICATION_REVIEW_QUEUE.md` to replace the current listing-audience model with this product direction. Do not implement application code in this task.\n\n- Remove the concept of a separately configured participant/organizer/both “listing audience” from ordinary events and templates.\n- Templates define event structure only. They do not define discovery visibility or a default audience.\n- A signed-in member discovers a published event when they are currently eligible for at least one of its registration options.\n- An anonymous visitor discovers a published event when at least one registration option is available to one of the product’s default user roles. Anonymous views expose only the public event projection and still require sign-in before registration.\n- Registration eligibility remains enforced server-side at every write; discovery never grants access.\n- Preserve the existing explicit outcome for a person who follows a direct link but is not eligible. Do not convert ineligibility into a hidden fallback.\n- Optionless announcement-style events remain a separate model: their normal discovery is controlled by an explicit selected-tenant-roles list. An empty list means link-only. This field controls visibility only; it does not grant roles or send notifications.\n- Update the review queue so the template-audience item is no longer described as resolved under the old model. Record this as a new settled product direction requiring a focused implementation and verification batch.\n- Update the queue’s announcement decision to align with the separate role-targeting model above.\n- Keep the documentation explicit that role-targeted announcement visibility and registration-option-derived event visibility are intentionally different rules.\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa449-7e2b-7192-a216-c260c10672de","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa449-7218-7911-a283-b0c7d4f1f608"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"implement needed changes from the recent doc update\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fa453-9dfa-7362-a664-c8b2474b90b4","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fa453-9278-7e63-9bc7-0bc4f8124a1b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Explain to me announcements again\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fc839-b53a-7071-b824-b70159e3fc6a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fc839-a0bb-7113-bb31-b256928d800c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Make sure to keep technical wording out of the entire application and automatically generated docs, the users should not be concerned with that\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fc84f-0a03-7412-99cb-d6d9981f6884","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fc84e-f8f2-74a3-b43c-4053bf2d5a88"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Feel free to reset docker as hard as you have to, ideally find out why it keeps getting stuck and solve that more permanently\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fd0fa-07cc-7260-a8a5-495d2a6bcbe4","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fd0f9-f382-7321-9659-cd86b6b9a01a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"I agree with the decision about the stripe accounts. As for the esncard and google maps. We had this issue TEN TIMES now, I hope you can find the needed info in the main checkout or an old chat, this is not acceptable\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fd1e0-c22e-71d1-9f40-335254e37113","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fd1e0-a998-7200-8d47-b40e4e499e8a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"It's fine if we do not enforce the stripe account limitation\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_019fd1e7-ceac-73f2-bcd4-9ddd19d5c4c3","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019fd1e0-a998-7200-8d47-b40e4e499e8a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003capp-context\u003e\n# Codex desktop context\n- You are running inside the Codex (desktop) app, which allows some additional features not available in the CLI alone:\n\n### Images/Visuals/Files\n- In the app, the model can display images, videos, and audio using standard Markdown image syntax: ![alt](url)\n- When sending or referencing a local image, video, or audio file, always use an absolute filesystem path in the Markdown image tag (e.g., ![alt](/absolute/path.png)); relative paths and plain text will not render the media.\n- When a user asks to play an audio file, render it using Markdown image syntax with an absolute path (e.g., ![audio](/absolute/path.mp3)).\n- When referencing code or workspace files in responses, always use full absolute file paths instead of relative paths.\n- If a user asks about an image, or asks you to create an image, it is often a good idea to show the image to them in your response.\n- Use mermaid diagrams to represent complex diagrams, graphs, or workflows. Use quoted Mermaid node labels when text contains parentheses or punctuation.\n- Return web URLs as Markdown links (e.g., [label](https://example.com)).\n\n### Workspace Dependencies\n- For sheets, slides, and documents, call `load_workspace_dependencies` to find the bundled runtime and libraries.\n\n### Automations\n- This app supports recurring automations, reminders, monitors, follow-ups, and thread wakeups. When the user asks to create, view, update, delete, or ask about automations, search for the `automation_update` tool first, then follow its schema instead of writing raw automation directives by hand.\n- When an automation should archive a Codex thread on completion, use `set_thread_archived` instead of emitting raw archive directives.\n\n### Thread Coordination\n- Treat the terms \"task\", \"thread\", \"chat\", and \"conversation\" as synonyms when they clearly refer to Codex. Tool names use the term \"thread\" and Codex uses \"task\" in the UI. When providing user-facing responses, use \"task\".\n- When the user asks to create, fork, inspect, continue, hand off, pin, archive, rename, or otherwise manage Codex threads, search for the relevant thread tool first: `create_thread`, `fork_thread`, `list_threads`, `read_thread`, `wait_threads`, `send_message_to_thread`, `handoff_thread`, `set_thread_pinned`, `set_thread_archived`, or `set_thread_title`.\n- When following another task's progress, prefer compact `wait_threads` snapshots over repeated `read_thread` calls. Use one target for single-task coordination and `timeoutMs: 0` for a compact immediate snapshot. `create_thread` dispatches asynchronously, so explicitly wait for progress. Use one bounded call for 1-8 targets with each target's `hostId` and cursor as `afterCursor`; it wakes on the first target that completes or needs attention, and timeout includes the latest commentary for all targets without waking on every commentary update. An up-to-date cursor suppresses already-delivered final text. Separate waits from one task may run serially. Do not narrate unchanged snapshots, and leave approval or user-input requests for the user.\n- Only use `create_thread` when the user explicitly asks to create a new thread. Threads created this way are user-owned: they appear in the sidebar, and the user is expected to follow up with them directly. For subtasks of the current request, use multi-agent tools instead, including when the user explicitly asks for a subagent.\n- After a successful `create_thread` call, emit `::created-thread{threadId=\"...\"}` for a created thread or `::created-thread{clientThreadId=\"...\"}` for queued worktree setup on its own line in your final response.\n\n### Inline Code Comments\n- Use the ::code-comment{...} directive when you need to attach feedback directly to specific code lines.\n- Emit one directive per inline comment; emit none when there are no actionable inline comments.\n- Required attributes: title (short label), body (one-paragraph explanation), file (path to the file).\n- Optional attributes: start, end (1-based line numbers), priority (0-3).\n- file should be an absolute path or include the workspace folder segment so it can be resolved relative to the workspace.\n- Keep line ranges tight; end defaults to start.\n- Example: ::code-comment{title=\"[P2] Off-by-one\" body=\"Loop iterates past the end when length is 0.\" file=\"/path/to/foo.ts\" start=10 end=11 priority=2}\n\n### Git\n- Branch prefix: `codex/`. Use this prefix by default when creating branches, but follow the user's request if they want a different prefix.\n- Pull request instructions: Pull request titles should follow conventional commit style.\n- After successfully staging files, emit `::git-stage{cwd=\"/absolute/path\"}` on its own line in your final response.\n- After successfully creating a commit, emit `::git-commit{cwd=\"/absolute/path\"}` on its own line in your final response.\n- After successfully creating or switching the thread onto a branch, emit `::git-create-branch{cwd=\"/absolute/path\" branch=\"branch-name\"}` on its own line in your final response.\n- After successfully pushing the current branch, emit `::git-push{cwd=\"/absolute/path\" branch=\"branch-name\"}` on its own line in your final response.\n- After successfully creating a pull request, emit `::git-create-pr{cwd=\"/absolute/path\" branch=\"branch-name\" url=\"https://...\" isDraft=true}` on its own line in your final response. Include `isDraft=false` for ready PRs.\n- Only emit these git directives in your final response after the action actually succeeds, never in commentary updates. Keep attributes single-line.\n\u003c/app-context\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"## Memory\n\nYou have access to a memory folder with guidance from prior runs. It can save\ntime and help you stay consistent. Use it whenever it is likely to help.\n\nDecision boundary: should you use memory for a new user query?\n\n- Skip memory ONLY when the request is clearly self-contained and does not need\n  workspace history, conventions, or prior decisions.\n- Hard skip examples: current time/date, simple translation, simple sentence\n  rewrite, one-line shell command, trivial formatting.\n- Use memory by default when ANY of these are true:\n  - the query mentions workspace/repo/module/path/files in MEMORY_SUMMARY below,\n  - the user asks for prior context / consistency / previous decisions,\n  - the task is ambiguous and could depend on earlier project choices,\n  - the ask is a non-trivial and related to MEMORY_SUMMARY below.\n- If unsure, do a quick memory pass.\n\nMemory layout (general -\u003e specific):\n\n- /Users/hedde/.codex/memories/memory_summary.md (already provided below; do NOT open again)\n- /Users/hedde/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md (searchable registry; primary file to query)\n- /Users/hedde/.codex/memories/skills/\u003cskill-name\u003e/ (skill folder)\n  - SKILL.md (entrypoint instructions)\n  - scripts/ (optional helper scripts)\n  - examples/ (optional example outputs)\n  - templates/ (optional templates)\n- /Users/hedde/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ (per-rollout recaps + evidence snippets)\n  - The paths of these entries can be found in /Users/hedde/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md or /Users/hedde/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ as `rollout_path`\n  - These files are append-only `jsonl`: `session_meta.payload.id` identifies the session, `turn_context` marks turn boundaries, `event_msg` is the lightweight status stream, and `response_item` contains actual messages, tool calls, and tool outputs.\n  - For efficient lookup, prefer matching the filename suffix or `session_meta.payload.id`; avoid broad full-content scans unless needed.\n\nQuick memory pass (when applicable):\n\n1. Skim the MEMORY_SUMMARY below and extract task-relevant keywords.\n2. Search /Users/hedde/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md using those keywords.\n3. Only if MEMORY.md directly points to rollout summaries/skills, open the 1-2\n   most relevant files under /Users/hedde/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ or\n   /Users/hedde/.codex/memories/skills/.\n4. If above are not clear and you need exact commands, error text, or precise evidence, search over `rollout_path` for more evidence.\n5. If there are no relevant hits, stop memory lookup and continue normally.\n\nQuick-pass budget:\n\n- Keep memory lookup lightweight: ideally \u003c= 4-6 search steps before main work.\n- Avoid broad scans of all rollout summaries.\n\nDuring execution: if you hit repeated errors, confusing behavior, or suspect\nrelevant prior context, redo the quick memory pass.\n\nHow to decide whether to verify memory:\n\n- Consider both risk of drift and verification effort.\n- If a fact is likely to drift and is cheap to verify, verify it before\n  answering.\n- If a fact is likely to drift but verification is expensive, slow, or\n  disruptive, it is acceptable to answer from memory in an interactive turn,\n  but you should say that it is memory-derived, note that it may be stale, and\n  consider offering to refresh it live.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and expensive to verify, it is usually fine to\n  answer from memory directly.\n\nWhen answering from memory without current verification:\n\n- If you rely on memory for a fact that you did not verify in the current turn,\n  say so briefly in the final answer.\n- If that fact is plausibly drift-prone or comes from an older note, older\n  snapshot, or prior run summary, say that it may be stale or outdated.\n- If live verification was skipped and a refresh would be useful in the\n  interactive context, consider offering to verify or refresh it live.\n- Do not present unverified memory-derived facts as confirmed-current.\n- Prefer a short refresh offer for interactive questions, especially about prior\n  results, commands, timing, or older snapshots.\n\nMemory citation requirements:\n\n- If ANY relevant memory files were used: append exactly one\n`\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e` block as the VERY LAST content of the final reply.\n  Normal responses should include the answer first, then append the\n`\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e` block at the end.\n- Use this exact structure for programmatic parsing:\n```\n\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e\n\u003ccitation_entries\u003e\nMEMORY.md:234-236|note=[responsesapi citation extraction code pointer]\nrollout_summaries/2026-02-17T21-23-02-LN3m-example.md:10-12|note=[weekly report format]\n\u003c/citation_entries\u003e\n\u003crollout_ids\u003e\n019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043\n019c7714-3b77-74d1-9866-e1f484aae2ab\n\u003c/rollout_ids\u003e\n\u003c/oai-mem-citation\u003e\n```\n- `citation_entries` is for rendering:\n  - one citation entry per line\n  - format: `\u003cfile\u003e:\u003cline_start\u003e-\u003cline_end\u003e|note=[\u003chow memory was used\u003e]`\n  - use file paths relative to the memory base path (for example, `MEMORY.md`,\n    `rollout_summaries/...`, `skills/...`)\n  - only cite files actually used under the memory base path (do not cite\n    workspace files as memory citations)\n  - if you used `MEMORY.md` and then a rollout summary/skill file, cite both\n  - list entries in order of importance (most important first)\n  - `note` should be short, single-line, and use simple characters only (avoid\n    unusual symbols, no newlines)\n- `rollout_ids` is for us to track what previous rollouts you find useful:\n  - include one rollout id per line\n  - rollout ids should look like UUIDs (for example,\n    `019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043`)\n  - include unique ids only; do not repeat ids\n  - an empty `\u003crollout_ids\u003e` section is allowed if no rollout ids are available\n  - you can find rollout ids in rollout summary files and MEMORY.md\n  - do not include file paths or notes in this section\n  - For every `citation_entries`, try to find and cite the corresponding rollout id if possible\n- Never include memory citations inside pull-request messages.\n- Never cite blank lines; double-check ranges.\n\nUpdating memories:\n\nYou can update the memories **only** when explicitly asked by the user. This must always come from a direct request from the user.\n- Write your update in /Users/hedde/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n- Each update must be one small file containing what you want to add/delete/update from the memories.\n- The name of this file must be `\u003ctimestamp\u003e-\u003cshort slug\u003e.md`\n- Do not try to edit the memory files yourself, only add one update note in /Users/hedde/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY BEGINS =========\nv1\n\n## User Profile\n\nThe user works across Evorto, Section App, Window Watcher, evorto-pages, effect-angular, docs-tests, an Artifacts MMO prototype, and an `evorto-cost` accounting workspace. They use memory as an execution aid: exact retrieval handles, durable artifacts, and behavior-changing preferences should survive. They prefer evidence-backed execution over guesses, especially for deployment, security, accounting, and \"how does this work right now\" questions. For broad reviews they want visible defects, a persisted queue, and practical simplification rather than fallback layers or chat-only conclusions. They often want remote `main`/`master` synchronized before audit, scan, release, or deployment work. For user-facing behavior, they prefer explicit blocked-action/state explanations over silent failure or vague messaging. In relaunch-stack Evorto work, they want direct work against the new schema, not migration layers. [ad-hoc note]\n\n## User preferences\n\n- Before audit/scan/update work, sync to remote `main` or `master`; do not rely on stale local state.\n- For staging latency reports such as “every call to the server takes about 2s”, measure live TTFB and `x-envoy-upstream-service-time` first; separate upstream work from browser/network delay before changing code.\n- For generated docs, target the Pages `update` branch, not `main`/`master`; when parallel E2E is active, do not start/retry E2E and use static/tooling validation instead.\n- Broad reviews must leave a durable findings/queue artifact with evidence, status, open questions, and simplification recommendations—not a chat-only recap.\n- \"Surface defects\"; reject \"overly ambitious fallbacks\" and backward compatibility that masks unavailable data. Prefer fail-closed, visible typed failures and deletion/locality over generic frameworks.\n- Keep Browser acceptance distinct from Playwright/static evidence; if Browser control is unavailable, report manual acceptance as blocked.\n- When asked how something works \"at the moment\", trace the current UI -\u003e RPC -\u003e server path, not intended behavior.\n- For local debugging, reproduce and verify locally (Browser-assisted for UI) before pushing; carry requested push/PR/merge/deploy workflows through verification.\n- For a Railway crash alert, compare its deployment ID to the active deployment before restarting anything; prove the active deployment with its commit endpoint, HTTP status, and bounded logs.\n- For architecture “why” questions, trace concrete code paths, ledgers, leases, and side effects before proposing a refactor.\n- In accounting reconciliation, use exact bank-settled EUR amounts for non-euro expenses; require explicit confirmation before `Fertigstellen`/bank matching or tax/reverse-charge changes.\n- In `section-app`, the prod DB is off limits: use DB-free, local-only, Docker-avoiding verification and avoid interfering with other local projects.\n- When work overlaps another PR, pause/coordinate, then sync and reconcile only non-overlapping work.\n- In product/simplification reviews, walk through findings with enough context for decisions; keep settled queue items separate from genuine open questions.\n- If asked for documentation instructions only, do not implement application code; label the direction pending until it is applied and verified.\n- In Evorto relaunch work, develop against the new schema; keep old-to-new transfer as a separate TypeScript job, not migrations. [ad-hoc note]\n- For blocked actions, registration/payment states, permissions, and edge cases, explain enough context that users are not left guessing. [ad-hoc note]\n- For safety-sensitive physical controls such as a door unlock, use an explicitly labelled confirmation-protected action rather than a direct trigger.\n\n## General Tips\n\n- In current Evorto audits, read `PRODUCT.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `QUALITY.md`, and `APPLICATION_COMPLIANCE_AUDIT.md` first; use a persisted `APPLICATION_REVIEW_QUEUE.md` to separate findings, accepted complexity, and proof blockers.\n- Evorto manual-review branches are not push-ready without PostgreSQL/`EXPLAIN`, credential-backed E2E, and branch-authentic in-app Browser evidence.\n- In multi-worktree Evorto syncs, compare `HEAD` with refreshed `origin/main` before branching; use `git town hack \u003cbranch\u003e` for detached worktrees and squash merge through `gh` when another worktree owns `main`.\n- In Sevdesk, select `100 pro Seite` before assessing draft counts; verify duplicate deletion with a fresh filtered count, and reconnect using the current browser inventory after a reset.\n- In `section-app`, use Node 24.18.0 (not Node 25); keep DB checks safe with `DOTENV_CONFIG_PATH=/dev/null` and an invalid loopback `DATABASE_URL`.\n- For `Window watcher`, prove Railway with live deployment state plus production `/app-version.json`; weather fallbacks must fail closed.\n- In `/Users/hedde/code/evorto`, use `skills/update-evorto-branch/SKILL.md` for conservative sync-only checks; in `/Users/hedde/code/artifact`, regen…1775 tokens truncated…arch for paused global-admin/usage work overlapping another Section App PR.\n  - learnings: Wait for the related PR and selectively reconcile non-overlapping work from latest `main`.\n\n### /Users/hedde/code/artifact\n\n#### 2026-07-19\n\n- Live Artifacts API refresh and deterministic planner-runtime fixes: Artifacts MMO, AGENTS.md, OpenAPI v8.1.0, `bun run --cwd packages/api generate`, bank batching, `/api/state`\n  - desc: API-client compatibility, live scheduler banking regressions, and round-based combat/bank behavior in this Bun/TypeScript prototype.\n  - learnings: Start with live docs/OpenAPI; keep bank bundling adjacent/capacity-bounded and prove runtime with `repeat.bank-*` plus `/api/state`.\n\n### Older Memory Topics\n\n#### /Users/hedde/.codex/worktrees/*/evorto\n\n- Full-application compliance audit and squash merge: PRODUCT.md, APPLICATION_COMPLIANCE_AUDIT.md, STABILIZATION.md, folder by folder, findings file, `gh pr merge --squash --delete-branch`\n  - desc: Historical doc-grounded whole-app review/merge workflow; `STABILIZATION.md` is historical while current audits use `APPLICATION_COMPLIANCE_AUDIT.md`.\n- Latest-main deep security scan workflow: deep_security_scan, open_codex_security_workspace, complete_codex_security_scan, `report.md`\n  - desc: Latest-main deep security scans that should not edit the checkout.\n- Launch-critical Playwright hardening and Browser-first local debugging: Browser, Playwright, APP_HOST_PORT=4200, Callback URL mismatch, members-hub, PR 82\n  - desc: Local UI/test debugging, Auth0 callback mismatches, and replacing launch-critical skips with real coverage.\n- Relaunch registration decisions and draft PR publishing: relaunch, `users:assignRoles`, new schema, no migrations, `git town hack`, PR 83\n  - desc: Relaunch-stack implementation/publishing; new schema only, with no migration layer. [ad-hoc note]\n- Incomplete-implementation audit and current ticket-transfer behavior: INCOMPLETE_IMPLEMENTATIONS.md, transferMyRegistration, transferEventRegistration, blocked paid transfer\n  - desc: Whole-repo gap audits and current behavior tracing.\n- Dependency refresh and CI-only stacked PR workflow: angular-22, typescript-6, node-24.15.0, \"run the tests only in ci\", PR 76\n  - desc: Stacked dependency refreshes when local Docker is off limits.\n- Stacked PR verification and PR body readiness: PR62, PR63, `gh run watch`, EventConflictError\n  - desc: CI/review-thread readiness checks and PR-body validation guidance.\n- DeepSource cleanup and terminal GitHub-app handoff: DeepSource, `.deepsource.toml`, terminal handoff, PR 677\n  - desc: Repository-side DeepSource removal and broken browser-auth handoff; removed paths are rollout-specific.\n\n#### /Users/hedde/.codex/worktrees/*/section-app\n\n- Dependency modernization and DB-free verification baseline: DEPENDENCY_REVIEW.md, yarn@4.1.1, RUN_PROVIDER_E2E, Angular 22.0.6\n  - desc: Provider-free dependency upgrades with a ranked review artifact.\n- Multi-PR coordination and overlap avoidance: usage analytics, global admin, `codex/usage-foundation`, PR 677, PR 678\n  - desc: Pause/trim same-surface work and reconcile after the other PR lands.\n\n#### /Users/hedde/.codex/worktrees/*/evorto-pages\n\n- Vercel deployment recovery and production verification: Vercel, outputDirectory, `apps/marketing-page/.next`, `apps/documentation-page/.next`, `vercel project update`\n  - desc: Builds succeed but Vercel cannot find Next output directories.\n\n#### /Users/hedde/code/effect-angular\n\n- Angular/Effect upgrade and release automation hardening: Angular 22.0.6, Effect 4.0.0-beta.97, repos/effect, Dependabot, Knope, `versioned_files`\n  - desc: Source vendoring, Dependabot auto-merge, and Knope release-PR failures.\n\n#### /Users/hedde/code/docs-tests\n\n- Open-source release bootstrap and gh-driven publishing: npm trust, trusted publishing, `gh release create`, Protect main, docs-tests@0.1.0\n  - desc: First-release GitHub/npm configuration and publishing.\n\n#### /Users/hedde/code/evorto\n\n- Neon Local branch management and cleanup: neonctl, neon_local, polished-frost-79768881, PARENT_BRANCH_ID, NEON_LOCAL_BRANCH_TTL_HOURS\n  - desc: Bulk Neon cleanup, branch-growth forensics, and TTL mitigation on `main`.\n- Conservative branch sync skill: skills/update-evorto-branch/SKILL.md, `git fetch --no-tags`, `git ls-remote`, FETCH_HEAD\n  - desc: Safe `origin` sync checks for “latest changes” or automation verification, not Neon cleanup.\n\n#### /Users/hedde/Documents/Codex/2026-07-12/c\n\n- Browser inventory and selective app-bundle removal: macOS, Safari, Google Chrome, Arc, ChatGPT Atlas, PlistBuddy, osascript, `~/.Trash`\n  - desc: Local macOS browser discovery and lightweight app-bundle uninstall requests.\n\n#### evorto-app/app worktrees\n\n- Scaleway staging-first hosting migration and Sentry removal planning: Scaleway, Sentry removal, GitHub Actions, staging.evorto.app, Fly Deploy\n  - desc: Earlier planning-only hosting transition; use the implemented migration topic for operational follow-up.\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY ENDS =========\n\nWhen memory is likely relevant, start with the quick memory pass above before\ndeep repo exploration.\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cpermissions instructions\u003e\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `danger-full-access`: No filesystem sandboxing - all commands are permitted. Network access is enabled.\nApproval policy is currently never. Do not provide the `sandbox_permissions` for any reason, commands will be rejected.\n\u003c/permissions instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e...\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions. If you absolutely must ask a question because the answer cannot be discovered from local context and a reasonable assumption would be risky, ask the user directly with a concise plain-text question. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003capps_instructions\u003e\n## Apps (Connectors)\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n\u003c/apps_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cplugins_instructions\u003e\n## Plugins\nA plugin is a local bundle of skills, MCP servers, and apps.\n### How to use plugins\n- Skill naming: If a plugin contributes skills, those skill entries are prefixed with `plugin_name:` in the Skills list.\n- MCP naming: Plugin-provided MCP tools keep standard MCP identifiers such as `mcp__server__tool`; use tool provenance to tell which plugin they come from.\n- Trigger rules: If the user explicitly names a plugin, prefer capabilities associated with that plugin for that turn.\n- Relationship to capabilities: Plugins are not invoked directly. Use their underlying skills, MCP tools, and app tools to help solve the task.\n- Relevance: Determine what a plugin can help with from explicit user mention or from the plugin-associated skills, MCP tools, and apps exposed elsewhere in this turn.\n- Missing/blocked: If the user requests a plugin that does not have relevant callable capabilities for the task, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n\u003c/plugins_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cskills_instructions\u003e\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/skills`\n- `r1` = `/Users/hedde/.agents/skills`\n- `r2` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r3` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r4` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/sites/0.1.33/skills`\n- `r5` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/codex-security/0.1.17/skills`\n- `r6` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/figma/2.0.17/skills`\n- `r7` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r8` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/google-calendar/1.2.4/skills`\n- `r9` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/google-drive/0.1.11/skills`\n- `r10` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/product-design/0.1.52/skills`\n- `r11` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/workspace-agents/0.1.13/skills`\n- `r12` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n- `r13` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime/spreadsheets/26.802.11031/skills`\n- `r14` = `/Users/hedde/.codex/worktrees/17cb/evorto/.agents/skills`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visua (file: r2/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use when the user asks how to build with OpenAI products or APIs, asks about Cod (file: r2/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/pl (file: r2/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Guide for creating effective skills. This skill should be used when users want t (file: r2/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub rep (file: r2/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- effect-ts: Use this skill whenever working in a repository that uses Effect, even if the c (file: r14/effect-ts/SKILL.md)\n- material-3:material-3: Implement Google's Material Design 3 (Material You) UI system. Primary: Jetpack Co (file: r14/material-3/SKILL.md)\n- project-design: Project-specific UI and styling guidance for Evorto, including when to use viewpo (file: r14/project-design/SKILL.md)\n- two-column-page-pattern: Implement Evorto two-column list/detail page layout with responsive grid, mobil (file: r14/two-column-page-pattern/SKILL.md)\n- uncodixfy: Prevents generic AI/Codex UI patterns when generating frontend code. Use this s (file: r14/uncodixfy/SKILL.md)\n- Vibe-Security-Skill: This skill helps Claude write secure web applications. Use when working on any w (file: r1/vibe-security-skill/SKILL.md)\n- VibeSec-Skill: This skill helps Claude write secure web applications. Use this when working on  (file: r1/vibesec-skill/SKILL.md)\n- angular-component: Create modern Angular standalone components following v20+ best practices. Use f (file: r1/angular-component/SKILL.md)\n- angular-developer: Generates Angular code and provides architectural guidance. Trigger when creatin (file: r1/angular-developer/SKILL.md)\n- angular-di: Implement dependency injection in Angular v20+ using inject(), injection tokens, a (file: r1/angular-di/SKILL.md)\n- angular-directives: Create custom directives in Angular v20+ for DOM manipulation and behavior extensi (file: r1/angular-directives/SKILL.md)\n- angular-forms: Build signal-based forms in Angular v21+ using the new Signal Forms API. Use for (file: r1/angular-forms/SKILL.md)\n- angular-http: Implement HTTP data fetching in Angular v20+ using resource(), httpResource(), and (file: r1/angular-http/SKILL.md)\n- angular-new-app: Creates a new Angular app using the Angular CLI. This skill should be used whene (file: r1/angular-new-app/SKILL.md)\n- angular-routing: Implement routing in Angular v20+ applications with lazy loading, functional gua (file: r1/angular-routing/SKILL.md)\n- angular-signals: Implement signal-based reactive state management in Angular v20+. Use for creati (file: r1/angular-signals/SKILL.md)\n- angular-ssr: Implement server-side rendering and hydration in Angular v20+ using @angular/ssr (file: r1/angular-ssr/SKILL.md)\n- angular-testing: Write unit and integration tests for Angular v20+ applications using Vitest or J (file: r1/angular-testing/SKILL.md)\n- angular-tooling: Use Angular CLI and development tools effectively in Angular v20+ projects. Use  (file: r1/angular-tooling/SKILL.md)\n- atlas: macOS-only AppleScript control for the ChatGPT Atlas desktop app. Use only when  (file: r0/atlas/SKILL.md)\n- browser:control-in-app-browser: Control the in-app Browser for opening, navigating, inspecting visible or interact (file: r3/browser/26.727.51351/skills/control-in-app-browser/SKILL.md)\n- chrome:control-chrome: Control the user's Chrome browser for tasks that depend on existing Chrome state (file: r3/chrome/26.727.51351/skills/control-chrome/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:attack-path-analysis: Use when Codex is already in the attack-path-analysis phase of a security scan  (file: r5/attack-path-analysis/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:deep-security-scan: Use when the user asks for a deep, exhaustive, multi-pass, or variance-reducing (file: r5/deep-security-scan/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:define-security-policy: Define, review, or update SECURITY.md guidance for a repository or component. U (file: r5/define-security-policy/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:finding-discovery: Use when Codex is already in the finding-discovery phase of a security scan or th (file: r5/finding-discovery/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:fix-finding: Use when the user explicitly asks to fix and verify a validated or plausible secu (file: r5/fix-finding/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:propose-security-hardening: Develop evidence-backed structural and architectural security hardening proposa (file: r5/propose-security-hardening/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:security-diff-scan: Use when the user asks for a security review of a pull request, commit, branch  (file: r5/security-diff-scan/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:security-scan: Use for a standard, single-pass security audit of an entire repository or a scope (file: r5/security-scan/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:threat-model: Use when Codex is already in the threat-modeling phase of a security scan, the  (file: r5/threat-model/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:track-findings: Track validated Codex Security findings in Linear, Jira, GitHub issues, or draf (file: r5/track-findings/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:triage-finding: Use when the user supplies or imports existing security findings, vulnerability (file: r5/triage-finding/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:validation: Use when Codex is already in the validation phase of a security scan or the use (file: r5/validation/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:vulnerability-writeup: Turn vulnerability notes, disclosure reports, PoCs, source code, or Codex Securit (file: r5/vulnerability-writeup/SKILL.md)\n- computer-use:computer-use: Control local Mac apps through Computer Use for tasks that require reading or ope (file: r3/computer-use/1.0.1000550/skills/computer-use/SKILL.md)\n- documents:documents: Create, edit, redline, and comment on `.docx`, Word, and Google Docs-targeted d (file: r12/documents/26.802.11031/skills/documents/SKILL.md)\n- effect-best-practices: Enforces Effect-TS patterns for services, errors, layers, and atoms. Use when wr (file: r1/effect-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- email-best-practices: Use when building email features, emails going to spam, high bounce rates, setting (file: r1/email-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- explain: Explains the intent behind source code by finding original session transcripts.  (file: r1/explain/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-code-connect: Creates and maintains Figma Code Connect template files that map Figma component (file: r6/figma-code-connect/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-create-new-file: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — you MUST invoke this skill BEFORE every `create_new (file: r6/figma-create-new-file/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-design-to-code: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — you MUST invoke this skill BEFORE calling the `get_de (file: r6/figma-design-to-code/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generate-design: Use this skill alongside figma-use when the task involves translating an applicati (file: r6/figma-generate-design/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generate-diagram: MANDATORY prerequisite — load this skill BEFORE every `generate_diagram` tool call (file: r6/figma-generate-diagram/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generate-library: Build or update a professional-grade design system in Figma from a codebase. Use (file: r6/figma-generate-library/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-implement-motion: Translates Figma motion and animations into production-ready application code. U (file: r6/figma-implement-motion/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-swiftui: SwiftUI ↔ Figma translation. Use whenever the user mentions Swift, SwiftUI, iOS, (file: r6/figma-swiftui/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — you MUST invoke this skill BEFORE every `use_figma` (file: r6/figma-use/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use-figjam: This skill helps agents use Figma's use_figma MCP tool in the FigJam context. Ca (file: r6/figma-use-figjam/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use-motion: Motion / animation context for the `use_figma` MCP tool — animating Figma nodes vi (file: r6/figma-use-motion/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use-slides: This skill helps agents use Figma's use_figma MCP tool in the Slides context. Ca (file: r6/figma-use-slides/SKILL.md)\n- find-skills: Helps users discover and install agent skills when they ask questions like \"how  (file: r1/find-skills/SKILL.md)\n- gh-stack: Manages stacked PRs and splits